            {
              "id": "CVE-2018-12115-node",
              "name": "StockMatcherCpeMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2018-12115 high vulnerability for node package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In all versions of Node.js prior to 6.14.4, 8.11.4 and 10.9.0 when used with UCS-2 encoding (recognized by Node.js under the names `'ucs2'`, `'ucs-2'`, `'utf16le'` and `'utf-16le'`), `Buffer#write()` can be abused to write outside of the bounds of a single `Buffer`. Writes that start from the second-to-last position of a buffer cause a miscalculation of the maximum length of the input bytes to be written."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/anchore/grype",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2018-12115\nSeverity: high\nPackage: node\nVersion: 10.8.0\nFix Version: 10.9.0,6.14.4,8.11.4\nType: binary\nLocation: /usr/local/bin/node\nData Namespace: nvd:cpe\nLink: [CVE-2018-12115](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2018-12115)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2018-12115**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| high  | node  | 10.8.0  | 10.9.0,6.14.4,8.11.4  | binary  | /usr/local/bin/node  | nvd:cpe  | [CVE-2018-12115](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2018-12115)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2018-12121-node",
              "name": "StockMatcherCpeMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2018-12121 high vulnerability for node package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Node.js: All versions prior to Node.js 6.15.0, 8.14.0, 10.14.0 and 11.3.0: Denial of Service with large HTTP headers: By using a combination of many requests with maximum sized headers (almost 80 KB per connection), and carefully timed completion of the headers, it is possible to cause the HTTP server to abort from heap allocation failure. Attack potential is mitigated by the use of a load balancer or other proxy layer."
              },
